[h1]Flavorium Universalis v0.3.12: A Career at Court[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.3.* | Multiplayer synchronized[/i]

[hr][/hr]

The cabinet used to be a slot machine. You appointed someone, waited, and events occasionally handed them a trait, with no relation to what that minister had actually spent their life doing. This release replaces that with a career: experience earned from the work, a specialisation that decides what a person can become, and things you can spend money on to steer it. There is a new Court panel to watch it happen in, a household of 43 offices to fill, and a good deal of court life hanging off the side of both.

[h2]Ministers now have a record[/h2]

Every minister earns experience in three separate tracks: administration, diplomacy and warfare. It comes from the duties you assign, the years they serve, and the wars, laws and royal marriages the realm carries through while they are in office. Two different things come out of it, and keeping them apart is the whole design.

[list]
[*] [b]Seniority[/b] is a level from 1 to 10 drawn from total experience. It measures what someone has been through, grants a growing bonus to how effectively they work, and gates what needs standing rather than skill: founding an institution, taking a pupil, presiding over an examination.
[*] [b]Specialisation[/b] is a tier from 0 to 3 in each track separately, drawn from that track's own experience. It measures what someone has learned to do, and it decides which traits are within reach.
[/list]

The two come apart constantly. A minister can be very senior and still a tier 0 soldier, because thirty years in the treasury teaches you nothing about supply. Worth listening to, worth keeping, and still not the person to put in charge of the army.

[h2]Careers, not chains[/h2]

Fifteen career ladders run through the trait pool. Each starts at a minor malus and ends in a mastery: a fumbling reformist becomes a law reformer, then a progressive reformist, then a master reformer. The entry rungs are deliberately mild negatives, because that is where a career starts rather than a punishment.

Progress is gated by the relevant tier, so a minister climbs only as fast as their experience in that discipline allows. Where a ladder forks you are asked which way it goes. Does your treasurer learn to invest, or learn to audit? Both answers are correct and they produce different ministers. The random trait events respect all of this now, so a green adjutant can no longer be handed a supreme command because a die came up well.

[h2]Four things to spend money on[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Postings.[/b] Send someone to the chancery, the embassy circuit or the staff college. The minister is unavailable while away and the experience arrives with them. An accomplished minister gets more out of a posting and flounders less often, so training compounds on talent rather than levelling everyone out.
[*] [b]Proteges.[/b] Take up to three people outside the cabinet into the crown's care. They can be trained, mentored and enrolled exactly like a serving minister, so a successor is ready before a seat opens. The nobility does not care for the crown noticing people it has not vouched for.
[*] [b]Mentorship.[/b] Put a senior minister's time at a junior's disposal for a decade. The student gains experience each year in whatever their mentor is strongest at, which steers a career rather than merely hurrying one. What was taught outlasts the teacher.
[*] [b]Court examinations.[/b] Sealed questions, answers read aloud, and a good deal of quiet reputational damage in a single afternoon. Everyone who sits it learns something. One of them does conspicuously better than expected, and it will not be the person presiding.
[/list]

[h2]Schools of thought[/h2]

A school is a standing institution. Its founder's discipline becomes the school's, and everyone enrolled thereafter grows in that discipline every year, in office or not. It keeps teaching after the founder is dead, which is the difference between an institution and a reputation.

A realm may hold three at most and they are a century apart by design. The first needs a university in your capital, the second the Scholarly Orders advance, the third Enlightened Administration. This is also what makes a protege worth having: someone enrolled spends the waiting years absorbing a discipline and arrives already pointed at its ladders.

[h2]The season, patronage, and residents abroad[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]An evening at court.[/b] Hiring the hall is the easy part. A night for the nobility is the most expensive and buys goodwill from people who already have it. Twelve chairs and no musicians gets some work done instead. Something always happens, and what kind depends on what you spent.
[*] [b]Patronage.[/b] A minister can put their name and money behind an artist for a generation. The crown gains prestige while it lasts and the minister gains diplomatic experience. The Patronage tab also sells a work discreetly: nobody is named, nothing is refused, and you get noticeably less for exactly that reason.
[*] [b]Secondment.[/b] Post a minister to one of your subjects as a resident. They grow considerably while there, and the relationship they build moves that subject's bond every year. An administrator deepens the political dimension, a diplomat the personal one, a soldier the military one.
[/list]

The estate delegation that turns up at a soiree is the cheapest route into the protege system, and mentorship reaches crown estate courtiers too, which opens a pool of people the mod previously ignored.

[h2]Running out of road[/h2]

A minister two decades at the top starts to run down. The work still gets done, it is simply taking longer and being checked more often. Send them to their estates for a year and manage without them, or keep them at the desk and accept the cost.

Two accomplished specialists of the same kind will circle each other, because there is one reputation available in that field and two people qualified to hold it. It turns to duplicated work and problems found in each other's proposals slightly too late to fix quietly. Back one, let them compete, or make them sign jointly and pay for it. And when a genuinely accomplished minister dies, whoever worked beside them keeps some of it.

All of this sits behind the [b]Cabinet Experience[/b] game rule. Turned off, ministers behave as they did before and the monthly pass over each court is skipped. Experience is tracked for your own court, for great powers, and for anything past 100 development.

[h2]A household of 43 offices[/h2]

The six household office traits could never coexist with a minister's career, because fourteen of the fifteen ladder entry traits exclude other cabinet traits. They were also unreachable after roughly 1500, since all six required an Age of Traditions advance.

An office is now a post rather than a trait: a country variable plus a modifier, opened by a vanilla advance and usually retired by a later one, so the household changes shape across the game instead of freezing in 1337. Each office explains itself before you appoint, with every modifier rendered the way the engine would render it, the requirement, and the scaling note. Appointment costs government power and the post carries an upkeep.

[h2]The bureaus are connected to something now[/h2]

The bureau system shipped isolated: implemented once, funded on a slider, and touched by nothing else. [b]Order vs Magnates[/b] generalises the Byzantine Taxis and Dynatoi axis into a societal value any realm with a bureaucracy slot can push. Order is the fisc, the chancery and the appointed official; Magnates are land, levies, and the men whose grandfathers held them. The Byzantine version stays for Byzantium, because Taxis and Dynatoi name specific fourteenth-century parties.

Bureaucracy upkeep now scales on economic base blended with tax base rather than on a flat figure.

[h2]Art[/h2]

145 icons drawn for traits, missions and banners, subject type icons, and bespoke societal value illustrations for both bureaucracy axes.

[h2]Fixes[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Bureaucracy maluses were not applying at all.[/b] 28 of 31 negative modifiers scaled on [i]scope:entrenchment[/i], which the bureaucracies readme documents but the engine never provides. The undefined read poisoned the enclosing scale, and a scale that evaluates to none does not soften a malus, it stops it applying. The engine already scales both boons and maluses by entrenchment on its own.
[*] [b]Seating a protege did not end their sponsorship.[/b] They stayed in the court panel, held one of three slots forever, and drew experience twice a year. Existing saves repair themselves at the next yearly tick.
[*] [b]A crown dependency could not be arranged by diplomacy.[/b] The royal marriage requirement sat where it hid the option on every path at once, and a union member cannot hold a royal marriage with its own union. It is now a stated requirement, waived for union members, and the size cap counts provinces rather than locations.
[*] [b]A cabinet event queued a follow-up that no longer existed[/b] after an events rewrite, breaking the refusal branch of A Request for Funds.
[*] [b]A cabinet action tooltip flooded the log[/b] every frame the cabinet tab was open, because vanilla omits a data context that its own neighbouring tooltip supplies.
[*] Ten prices now have their cost modifier types registered, all ten seniority tooltip keys exist rather than only the five in use, and a country capital name reads through the location that actually has one.
[*] A Request for Funds fired far too often. Its weight drops from 8 to 3 and it gains a six year cooldown on the realm rather than only on the minister.
[*] [b]The discreet art sale offered immovable property.[/b] Monuments, palaces, temple towers, regalia and mansions are no longer candidates, since none of them can be quietly carried out of the country.
[*] [b]The discreet sale paid too well.[/b] Its price is halved across the board, and halved again for anything below 70 quality, which is the threshold vanilla itself treats as the mark of a desirable work.
[/list]

[h2]Localization[/h2]

French, German and Spanish text is updated for all new and rewritten content.
